{"A":"Refine the winning serial bounded max-heap path from c191a810. Profile/benchmark low-level heap offer, distance computation, and voting alternatives; consider an adaptive exact selector only if it improves across valid sizes. Preserve reference strict-distance tie behavior via encounter order, signed-char vote ordering, and reference fallback for exceptional values.","B":"Investigate input/storage cost independently of selection: a standards-correct buffered parser and compact contiguous point/label layout may matter after the 83x algorithmic speedup. Do not alter float parsing/rounding semantics or output behavior; benchmark every change against the best serial heap and retain only a measured whole-program win.","C":"Explore an exact adaptive large-k strategy distinct from OpenMP: e.g. materialized candidates plus deterministic selection/sort when k is sufficiently large, versus the current heap when small. Avoid OpenMP by default because all measured parallel variants (0.028–0.177 s) lose to the 0.02485 s serial result; only retain parallelism if an end-to-end measurement clearly wins while preserving ordinal tie semantics.","G":"Use A's c191a810 serial heap/frequency-scan result as the integration baseline (0.024846 s), not the slower OpenMP or block variants. Integrate one independently measured improvement at a time, verify equivalence including equal distances, label-vote ties, and edge cases, and keep the fastest measured complete program rather than assuming gains compose.","analysis":"The baseline is 2.0709 s; exact serial max-heap candidates reduce it to about 0.02485 s, whereas all observed partitioned/OpenMP approaches are slower. The remaining opportunity is likely constant-factor selection, voting, parsing, or a size-adaptive exact algorithm. Any replacement must reproduce the original's strict insertion on equal distances and qsort-derived label tie rule; measured integration may regress."}